    <bpmn:process id="W_W1392" name="W1392. DC-Level Business Continuity Plan, Annual Tabletop Exercise &amp; Recovery Time Objective Validation" isExecutable="false">
      <bpmn:documentation>Trigger: Scheduled — annual BCP review and tabletop exercise; ad-hoc triggered by significant operational change (new DC opening, WMS upgrade, major equipment installation) or after actual disruption event
Frequency: Annual per DC; tabletop exercise conducted separately for each of the 4 DCs
Volume: 4 DCs × annual cycle = 4 exercises/year
Owner: DC Operations Manager
Participants: DC Operations Manager, DC Shift Supervisors, Supply Planning Manager, Transportation Manager, IT Infrastructure Manager, HR Manager, Safety Officer, VP Supply Chain (exercise observer)
Source: workflow W1392 — DC-Level Business Continuity Plan, Annual Tabletop Exercise &amp; Recovery Time Objective Validation</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:laneSet id="W1392_LaneSet4">
        <bpmn:lane id="W1392_Lane1" name="DC Ops Manager">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1392_Task6</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1392_Task7</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1392_Task9</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1392_Task10</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1392_Start5</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="W1392_Lane2" name="All Participants">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1392_Task8</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="W1392_Lane3" name="VP Supply Chain">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1392_Task11</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1392_End12</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
      </bpmn:laneSet>
      <bpmn:startEvent id="W1392_Start5" name="Scheduled — annual BCP review and tabletop exercise; ad-hoc triggered by significant…">
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1392_Flow15</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:startEvent>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1392_Task6" name="Annual BCP document review and update">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 1: Annual BCP document review and update (Q1 each year): DC Operations Manager reviews and updates DC-specific BCP: (a) Contact tree: updated phone numbers for all DC management, shift supervisors, key vendors (3PL carriers, equipment maintenance, IT support), emergency services; (b) Alternate DC routing: for each store served by this DC, identify alternate DC that can serve as backup (e.g., DC3 Laguna can backstop DC4 Clark for North/Central Luzon stores); alternate routing includes estimated additional transit time and cost; (c) Critical systems recovery: WMS recovery procedure (failover to DR site per W1322), RF device reconnection, label printer failover, EDI/API reconnection to stores and carriers; (d) Manual operations procedure: if WMS is down, paper-based pick/pack/ship process with pre-printed pick tickets and manual manifest generation; (e) Inventory recovery: procedure for verifying inventory accuracy after system outage — cycle count of critical SKUs before resuming operations; (f) Communication protocol: notify affected stores of delayed deliveries, notify carriers of modified pickup schedules, notify VP Supply Chain of disruption status
Responsible (R): DC Ops Manager
Accountable (A): VP Supply Chain
Duration: 8–16 hours/DC</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1392_Flow15</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1392_Flow16</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1392_Task7" name="Tabletop exercise scenario development">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 2: Tabletop exercise scenario development: DC Operations Manager develops realistic disruption scenario for tabletop exercise — scenarios rotate annually: (a) Year 1: Extended power outage (24+ hours) with generator failure; (b) Year 2: WMS system failure requiring DR failover; (c) Year 3: Fire in pick module destroying inventory and equipment; (d) Year 4: Severe flooding preventing inbound/outbound truck access for 72 hours; scenario includes timeline of events, decision points, and injects (new information introduced during exercise to test adaptability)
Responsible (R): DC Ops Manager
Accountable (A): VP Supply Chain
Duration: 4–8 hours/scenario</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1392_Flow16</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1392_Flow17</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1392_Task8" name="Tabletop exercise execution">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 3: Tabletop exercise execution (half-day session at DC): (a) DC Operations Manager briefs participants on scenario; (b) walk through recovery timeline: T+0 (disruption), T+1 hour, T+4 hours, T+8 hours, T+24 hours, T+72 hours; at each interval, participants describe their actions; (c) decision points tested: when to activate alternate DC routing? when to switch to manual operations? when to notify stores? how to prioritize outbound shipments when capacity is limited? (d) injects introduced: &quot;generator fuel supply runs out at T+12 hours&quot;, &quot;3PL carrier unable to pick up for 48 hours due to road closure&quot;, &quot;WMS DR failover takes 6 hours instead of expected 2 hours&quot;; (e) VP Supply Chain observes and takes notes on gaps and improvement areas
Responsible (R): All Participants
Accountable (A): DC Ops Manager
Duration: 4 hours</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1392_Flow17</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1392_Flow18</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1392_Task9" name="RTO validation">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 4: RTO validation: during and after exercise, measure projected recovery time against RTO targets: (a) Partial operations RTO (24 hours): could the DC resume even limited outbound shipments (top 50 stores, critical SKUs only) within 24 hours? What bottlenecks prevent faster recovery? (b) Full operations RTO (72 hours): could the DC resume normal throughput (~40–45 shipments/day per the Background basis) within 72 hours? What additional resources are needed? (c) document actual vs. projected recovery timeline with identified gaps
Responsible (R): DC Ops Manager
Accountable (A): VP Supply Chain
Duration: 2 hours (post-exercise)</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1392_Flow18</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1392_Flow19</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1392_Task10" name="Lessons learned and action items">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 5: Lessons learned and action items: DC Operations Manager compiles exercise findings: (a) recovery procedure gaps (steps that don&#x27;t work in practice), (b) resource gaps (equipment, staffing, vendor support needed but not pre-arranged), (c) communication failures (who wasn&#x27;t notified in time?), (d) system recovery issues (WMS failover slower than expected), (e) generate action items with owners and due dates; track to completion
Responsible (R): DC Ops Manager
Accountable (A): VP Supply Chain
Duration: 2 hours</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1392_Flow19</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1392_Flow20</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1392_Task11" name="Cross-DC learning">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 6: Cross-DC learning: VP Supply Chain consolidates lessons learned from all 4 DC exercises; shares best practices and common gaps across DCs; updates enterprise-wide BCP standards if systemic issues identified
Responsible (R): VP Supply Chain
Accountable (A): COO
Duration: 4 hours/year</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1392_Flow20</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1392_Flow21</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="W1392_End12" name="Complete">
        <bpmn:incoming>W1392_Flow21</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
